WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:23.600
Potatoes with 30% vermicompost in simulated lunar soil match Earth yields. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 258. Web caught a stellar merger in Andromeda. Engineers modeled how the moon shakes, then a cosmic deep dive on lunar seismic waves. Imeric Goyer of Oregon State University's Department of Botany and Plant Pathology, in Fizz.org Space coverage, said,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.940
We found that potatoes can complete their entire life cycle in simulated lunar soil when

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:32.440
supplemented with organic matter, and the harvest is safe to eat.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.780
Plants grown without that amendment were severely stunted.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:41.000
That added organic matter locks up heavy metals and cuts how much of those harmful compounds

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.080
the plants take in.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.940
The trials ran on five simulants of lunar mare and highland ground, plus soil from Hermiston

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:48.380
Oregon.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:57.820
The team picked potatoes for packing more calories into a plot than many other crops can, and a later paper will test 10 potato varieties in those same simulants.

00:00:58.826 --> 00:01:08.226
A stellar merger at the outskirts of the Andromeda galaxy, the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA, reported, produced a remnant nickname BOW.

00:01:08.566 --> 00:01:18.546
A star slightly less massive than the Sun, approximately 0.7 solar masses, spiraled into a super giant after only seven orbits lasting about seven years.

00:01:18.546 --> 00:01:24.586
Before the merger, the primary held seven times the sun's mass, stretched 350 times the

00:01:24.586 --> 00:01:27.806
sun's radius, and Sean 18,000 times brighter.

00:01:28.506 --> 00:01:33.186
Webb, a space telescope, looked at the system 47 days after the eruption peaked.

00:01:33.606 --> 00:01:35.686
The earliest follow-up of this kind of event.

00:01:36.326 --> 00:01:40.846
Mid-infrared readings let the team measure how much mass was thrown off during common envelope

00:01:40.846 --> 00:01:41.526
evolution.

00:01:42.026 --> 00:01:45.586
The phase when two stars share their outer layers as they spiral together.

00:01:45.586 --> 00:01:51.826
and they check those readings against ultraviolet, visible, and near infrared observations from multiple telescopes.

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:58.150
Civil engineers at Slate Geotechnical Consultants created, Fizz.org Space Noted,

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:04.670
the first ground motion model tailored to shallow moon quakes from Apollo seismic recordings and physics-based simulations.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:09.610
A ground motion model describes how shaking travels through the ground after a quake.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:14.350
It forecasts much stronger shaking far from the source than Earth-based models do.

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:20.450
At 200 km, shaking on the moon still counts as a short-to-moderate distance by lunar standards.

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:26.010
While that same span on Earth would already show mostly attenuated motion, and strong shaking

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:31.230
still shows up past 1000 km on the moon, even after small magnitude events.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:36.870
A colder, more solid interior lets seismic waves hold their energy, and a fractured surface

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.130
scatters that energy into long quotas.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:44.630
Apollo records put average strong shaking at 54 minutes, compared with under one minute

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.610
for shallow crustal earthquakes on Earth.

00:02:47.513 --> 00:02:52.573
Apollo's four station data set carried lunar values for temperature, rigidity, and surface

00:02:52.573 --> 00:02:54.093
roughness into the simulations.

00:02:54.773 --> 00:02:59.433
Limited station geometry from the Apollo era makes it harder to pinpoint the exact source

00:02:59.433 --> 00:02:59.993
location.

00:03:00.673 --> 00:03:05.153
Broadband instruments set at varied depths and distances would record a wider frequency

00:03:05.153 --> 00:03:10.393
range, and measure directly how wave energy fades with distance and time. That would

00:03:10.393 --> 00:03:14.793
tighten the model and hand engineers the figures required to design structures that can take

00:03:14.793 --> 00:03:16.053
prolonged lunar shaking.

00:03:17.127 --> 00:03:23.507
Korea Aerospace Administration, or KASA, in Asia reporting, held a workshop on numerical

00:03:23.507 --> 00:03:29.427
models of astrophysical phenomena and artificial intelligence, or AI, with about 50 researchers

00:03:29.427 --> 00:03:31.227
from universities and institutes.

00:03:31.227 --> 00:03:37.647
Participants presented simulations of galaxy formation, gravitational waves, planetary collisions,

00:03:38.007 --> 00:03:42.867
and the effects of lunar lander exhaust plumes on regolith, and discussed using these models

00:03:42.867 --> 00:03:47.807
to prevalidate lander safety and observation strategies for space telescopes and lunar

00:03:47.807 --> 00:03:48.827
surface instruments.

00:03:49.367 --> 00:03:54.447
The Korea Institute of Science and Technology Information and the Institute for Basic Science

00:03:54.447 --> 00:03:59.507
shared updates on national supercomputing resources, while the Korea Astronomy and Space

00:03:59.507 --> 00:04:03.587
Science Institute reported progress on an AI collaboration platform.

00:04:04.187 --> 00:04:08.527
KSA plans to incorporate the proposals into future mission planning and policy.

00:04:09.592 --> 00:04:13.812
Tough students joined Space Vision 2026 at NASA's Space Center Houston.

00:04:14.432 --> 00:04:19.632
The toughs daily reported, and met astronauts including Jim Weatherby and Dan Burbank, plus

00:04:19.632 --> 00:04:23.792
industry leaders involved in rocketry clubs and lunar land or design contests.

00:04:24.372 --> 00:04:28.912
Twelve students attended the two-and-a-half-day gathering, which ran networking, project

00:04:28.912 --> 00:04:34.972
team presentations and a keynote from former astronaut Joe Acaba who told students to stay curious

00:04:34.972 --> 00:04:40.292
and take pride in their work. People from schools that lack strong aerospace programs called it a

00:04:40.292 --> 00:04:45.861
rare chance to meet peers and professionals they would not otherwise encounter. Dragon's departure

00:04:45.861 --> 00:04:52.521
from the International Space Station, or ISS, SpaceX said, has a new target. The company is aiming

00:04:52.521 --> 00:04:57.941
for no earlier than Friday, November 20, and teams continue monitoring weather at the plan's

00:04:57.941 --> 00:05:04.399
flashdown locations. United Launch Alliance Space Flight now reported, said preliminary data

00:05:04.399 --> 00:05:09.579
from the November 15th Leo I launch, show the booster's low thrust came from two little

00:05:09.579 --> 00:05:15.879
fuel reaching the BE4 engines, not from the engines themselves. All six solid rocket boosters

00:05:15.879 --> 00:05:21.439
and the Centaur upper stage behaved as expected after earlier nozzle improvements. The Federal

00:05:21.439 --> 00:05:28.161
Aviation Administration, or FAA, is overseeing the investigation still underway. Empire State

00:05:28.161 --> 00:05:33.721
building glowed gold, NASA said, on November 17th to mark Voyager 1 reaching one light day

00:05:33.721 --> 00:05:39.121
from Earth. A distance no other human-made object has achieved. A light day is the distance

00:05:39.121 --> 00:05:45.681
light travels in a day. Voyager 1 launched in 1977 and still sends back data from interstellar

00:05:45.681 --> 00:05:52.594
space. European Space Agency or ESA, member states will meet in Rome on December 15th to

00:05:52.594 --> 00:05:57.994
decide Europe's next steps in space exploration. That covers today's Space and Science news.

